<article class="env-lemma" id="071B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/071B">Lemma <span data-tag="071B">20.38.1</span>.</a> In the situation described above. Denote $\mathcal{H}^ m = H^ m(\mathcal{F}^\bullet )$ the $m$th cohomology sheaf. Let $\mathcal{B}$ be a set of open subsets of $X$. Let $d \in \mathbf{N}$. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
every open in $X$ has a covering whose members are elements of $\mathcal{B}$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for every $U \in \mathcal{B}$ we have $H^ p(U, \mathcal{H}^ q) = 0$ for $p > d$ and $q < 0$<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000490693" id="a0000490693-mark"><sup>1</sup></a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then (<a href="/tag/071A" data-tag="071A">20.38.0.1</a>) is a quasi-isomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/070M" data-tag="070M">13.34.6</a> it suffices to show that the map $\mathcal{F}^\bullet \to R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits \tau _{\geq -n} \mathcal{F}^\bullet $ is an isomorphism. This is Lemma <a href="/tag/0D64" data-tag="0D64">20.37.9</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>